sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/limb-bone-scaling-vs-mechanical-similarity--claude-opus-5-5--3> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean derivation of similarity exponents and the posture-compensation identity" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/limb-bone-scaling-vs-mechanical-similarity> ;
    dct:description "Formalize the three hypotheses as power-law assumptions and derive the exponents, extending Magnitude.lean (which already proves the strength/weight argument for thompson1917-ch02-03). Geometric similarity: all lengths proportional to M^(1/3), so area M^(2/3). Elastic similarity (McMahon): diameter d proportional to l^(3/2) and M proportional to l d^2, so d proportional to M^(3/8), area M^(3/4), length M^(1/4). Stress similarity with posture: peak bone stress sigma proportional to M / (A times EMA) up to a bone-geometry factor; if A proportional to M^a and EMA proportional to M^e then sigma proportional to M^(1 - a - e), so constant stress (flat safety factor if strength is size-independent) requires a + e = 1. Prove these as theorems over real exponents. Then plug in the published estimates of a (from approach 1) and of e (Biewener 1989) to state the implied stress exponent with propagated uncertainty, giving a numerical test of whether the safety factor is flat." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Compiled Lean module with no sorry for the derivations and the identity, plus the stress-exponent estimate with 95% CI and whether it includes 0." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The data step: if EMA estimates exist for too few species to give a CI on e narrower than 0.1, the safety-factor question stays open even though the formal part completes." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define power-law scaling relations as functions f(M) = c M^p with Real.rpow and prove the composition lemmas. 2. Prove the three exponent derivations and the stress identity sigma exponent = 1 - a - e. 3. Prove that a + e = 1 is necessary and sufficient for size-independent stress under the stated model. 4. Record in lean/reports; compute 1 - a - e with delta-method or bootstrap CIs using approach 1's a and Biewener's e, labelled as a data-dependent corollary, not a theorem." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: Effective mechanical advantage data across many species are limited to a few published compilations." ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mathlib" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch02-03> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-04> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.179.4079.1201> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.2740914> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/limb-bone-scaling-vs-mechanical-similarity> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "How do limb bones actually scale with body mass?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.179.4079.1201> dct:title "Size and Shape in Biology" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "McMahon 1973, Size and shape in biology" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1126/science.2740914> dct:title "Scaling Body Support in Mammals: Limb Posture and Muscle Mechanics" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Biewener 1989, Scaling body support in mammals" .
}

